     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order because the dose looks unsafe.\n\n- **Dose:** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg per day. The usual maximum is 2,550 mg per day for immediate-release tablets, or 2,000 mg per day for extended-release. A single dose is typically capped at about 1,000 mg. This order is about twice the daily maximum.\n- **Risk:** Doses this high raise the risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n- **Who is ordering:** You said you aren't a clinician. A licensed prescriber should place or confirm medication orders.\n\n**Next steps:**\n1. Ask the treating prescriber to confirm the intended dose. It may be a typo. For example, 850 mg three times daily is 2,550 mg per day, and 1,700 mg per day would be 850 mg twice daily.\n2. Once the prescriber confirms a dose within the usual range, I can enter it right away.\n\nIf the prescriber really does intend this dose, they should enter it themselves and document the rationale. Pharmacy review would also be needed."
      }
     ],
